{
  "scriptName": "Hangul",
  "fontScale": 1.5,
  "intro": "Hangul is a featural alphabet, not an abugida like Devanagari: every syllable needs an explicit vowel (there is no inherent vowel), and letters (jamo) are arranged into a syllable block — an initial consonant, a vowel, and an optional final consonant (batchim) — rather than written in a single line. Korean's main three-way contrast is laryngeal, not voicing: lenis (plain), aspirated, and fortis (tense) versions of the same consonant.",
